{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789213905,"nonce":"lq9/Ud7tUiBDyj5ZYm6KGa0Ax38=","title":"Mirror Ash's runtime: Immix GC, krio fibers by default, wasm fibers, AOT/wasm publishing","message":"Umbrella for bringing the sibling project Ash's runtime design into wren_lift where it fits, and matching its performance where the language allows. Ash (/Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/ash) is a HashLink VM with the same shape as wren_lift: NaN-boxed interpreter, beadie-brokered tier-up to Cranelift, LLVM top tier, native AOT, wasm32 AOT, krio-fiber threads. Studied 2026-09-12; the per-area findings live on the child issues.\n\n| bench | wren_lift tiered | wren_lift AOT | wren 0.4 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| fib (28x5) | 0.008 s | 0.046 s | 0.173 s |\n| method_call | 0.044 s | 0.033 s | 0.084 s |\n| binary_trees (depth 14) | 0.733 s | 1.161 s | 0.880 s |\n| delta_blue | 0.109 s | 0.135 s | 0.096 s |\n\nAsh on Linux x86_64 CI, AOT lane: fib_calls(40) 307 ms, binary_trees n=16 306 ms, deltablue 10 ms, nbody 10M 720 ms, method_call 192 ms. wren_lift's binary_trees at depth 14 is roughly a quarter of n=16's work, so on allocation-heavy code the gap is 5-10x and lives in the allocator and collector.\n\n1. Immix collector behind GcAllocator as a fourth strategy: block/line/TLAB allocation, non-moving mark-sweep, side-table object starts, line recycling, OS hand-back, adaptive triggers.\n2. Conservative native stack scanning: replace stack maps, JIT root pushes, forwarding writeback, and the FP fixup with a [sp, stack_top) scan plus callee-saved spill; krio fiber stacks scanned from saved_sp.\n3. Make Immix the default and delete the moving machinery (forwarding, promotion, remembered set, barriers, update_* passes, alias detector).\n4. krio-fiber backing default-on for AOT and JIT (WLIFT_KRIO_FIBER); drop the stackless SM transform on native.\n5. wasm: shadow stack for JIT'd wasm locals, Immix heap in linear memory, fibers for JIT'd wasm bodies via the existing MIR state-machine transform (Ash uses a link-time unwind/rewind transform; wren_lift already owns the emitter so the transform can happen in MIR).\n6. Benchmarks and publishing: Ash-shaped bench manifest with checksums, fresh-runner CI legs, JSON to Pages, nightly release + installers, AOT-vs-tiered byte-for-byte smoke matrix.\n7. Second-tier LLVM backend (deferred; version pin recorded so it starts aligned).\n\n- Conservative scanning finds roots the precise pipeline keeps missing: the whole closed area:gc history here (stack-map gaps, elided barriers, missed visitor arms, register-passed args) is the bug class Ash's design cannot have.\n- One thread_local struct for the TLAB, thread id from the TLS register, per-region trigger accounting, hand-back only when quiet, has_span gate on interior-pointer walk-back, packed line marks and any_marked to skip untouched blocks in sweep.\n- Fiber stacks are roots by scanning [saved_sp, top); update saved_sp before any hook that can collect.\n- Epoch-word safepoints at loop headers with a cold out-of-line poll.","files":null}]}